1
00:00:00,308 --> 00:00:02,992
2% of you... is him.

2
00:00:03,334 --> 00:00:04,942
He's a Neanderthal.

3
00:00:05,026 --> 00:00:08,403
His people vanished
forty thousand years ago.

4
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:12,028
But if your family
comes from anywhere outside Africa,

5
00:00:12,095 --> 00:00:16,164
about 2% of your DNA
came from people like him.

6
00:00:16,291 --> 00:00:20,053
So why did they die out... and you didn't?

7
00:00:20,475 --> 00:00:26,331
Three hundred thousand years ago, at least
eight kinds of humans walked this planet.

8
00:00:26,398 --> 00:00:29,314
Today, only one is left.

9
00:00:29,494 --> 00:00:34,102
Were we smarter? Stronger?
Or did we kill them?

10
00:00:34,426 --> 00:00:37,513
The real answer is much stranger.

11
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:42,359
And it starts with a shock:
the first times we reached their lands...

12
00:00:42,426 --> 00:00:43,726
we lost.

13
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:47,981
Let's go back three hundred
thousand years.

14
00:00:48,188 --> 00:00:53,891
In Morocco, at Jebel Irhoud:
people with faces almost like yours.

15
00:00:53,958 --> 00:00:57,488
The oldest
members of our species ever found.

16
00:00:57,572 --> 00:01:00,488
In Zambia, a heavy-browed people.

17
00:01:00,726 --> 00:01:05,582
In 1921, miners dug
one of their skulls out of a mine.

18
00:01:05,649 --> 00:01:10,564
Ten of his teeth were rotten:
one of the oldest toothaches we know.

19
00:01:10,691 --> 00:01:14,009
In southern Africa, deep in a cave:

20
00:01:14,076 --> 00:01:18,794
Homo naledi, with a brain
less than half the size of yours.

21
00:01:18,861 --> 00:01:23,794
Their bones lay past a crack just seven
inches wide at its narrowest.

22
00:01:23,861 --> 00:01:27,025
Six scientists were chosen
for their small size,

23
00:01:27,092 --> 00:01:28,699
just to bring them out.

24
00:01:28,810 --> 00:01:32,589
In Europe and western Asia:
the Neanderthals.

25
00:01:32,656 --> 00:01:35,896
Short, powerful, built for the Ice Age.

26
00:01:35,963 --> 00:01:38,648
The people 2% of you came from.

27
00:01:38,735 --> 00:01:41,552
On Java: Homo erectus,

28
00:01:41,619 --> 00:01:45,573
a species that would last
almost two million years.

29
00:01:45,701 --> 00:01:49,231
And some of the strangest
lived on islands.

30
00:01:49,382 --> 00:01:53,392
On Flores:
people about three and a half feet tall,

31
00:01:53,459 --> 00:01:59,084
living among dwarf elephants, giant rats,
storks taller than they were...

32
00:01:59,151 --> 00:02:01,528
and Komodo dragons.

33
00:02:01,656 --> 00:02:04,320
And on Luzon, in the Philippines:

34
00:02:04,387 --> 00:02:10,264
another small human, with curved fingers,
as if still made for climbing trees.

35
00:02:10,443 --> 00:02:15,068
And somewhere in Asia,
a people whose very first trace...

36
00:02:15,135 --> 00:02:18,204
was a sliver of a child's little finger.

37
00:02:18,437 --> 00:02:21,831
Scientists still argue
over the exact number.

38
00:02:21,898 --> 00:02:26,122
Some count nine.
But at least eight kinds of humans.

39
00:02:26,195 --> 00:02:29,974
And then, one by one, they vanished.

40
00:02:30,041 --> 00:02:34,725
By about forty thousand years ago,
almost all of them were gone.

41
00:02:35,348 --> 00:02:39,358
The first answer that comes
to mind is the one we like best...

42
00:02:39,425 --> 00:02:40,956
we were smarter.

43
00:02:41,034 --> 00:02:44,026
But look at what Neanderthals could do.

44
00:02:44,111 --> 00:02:49,026
Their brains were as big as ours.
On average, even a little bigger.

45
00:02:49,106 --> 00:02:54,462
At least two hundred thousand years ago,
they were heating birch bark into glue,

46
00:02:54,529 --> 00:02:57,370
to fix stone points to wooden handles.

47
00:02:57,437 --> 00:03:00,583
They twisted plant fibers into string.

48
00:03:00,661 --> 00:03:01,730
And art?

49
00:03:01,969 --> 00:03:06,594
In a cave in France,
at least fifty-seven thousand years ago,

50
00:03:06,661 --> 00:03:09,423
they drew
lines in the wall with their fingers.

51
00:03:09,584 --> 00:03:12,961
The oldest engravings
we know from their hands.

52
00:03:13,197 --> 00:03:17,669
In a cave in Iraq lies a man
whose face had been crushed,

53
00:03:17,736 --> 00:03:21,728
whose right arm was useless,
and who could barely hear.

54
00:03:21,805 --> 00:03:26,874
Yet he lived into his forties.
A man like that doesn't survive alone.

55
00:03:27,035 --> 00:03:28,951
Someone looked after him.

56
00:03:29,184 --> 00:03:32,407
These weren't brutes. They were people.

57
00:03:32,738 --> 00:03:36,787
Which makes the question harder.
If they were strong,

58
00:03:36,854 --> 00:03:41,902
smart, and had survived ice
ages for hundreds of thousands of years...

59
00:03:41,969 --> 00:03:44,038
why did they lose to us?

60
00:03:44,665 --> 00:03:48,905
And this is where the story
we tell ourselves starts to crack.

61
00:03:48,972 --> 00:03:51,657
For a long time, they were winning.

62
00:03:51,835 --> 00:03:55,673
Our species tried to leave
Africa more than once.

63
00:03:55,751 --> 00:04:01,299
Two hundred and ten thousand years ago,
in Apidima cave in southern Greece,

64
00:04:01,366 --> 00:04:05,358
lived someone many scientists
consider one of us.

65
00:04:05,486 --> 00:04:08,804
The next
skull in that cave is a Neanderthal's.

66
00:04:08,871 --> 00:04:11,881
And our species doesn't turn
up in Europe again for over

67
00:04:11,948 --> 00:04:14,940
a hundred and fifty thousand years.

68
00:04:15,067 --> 00:04:17,308
A hundred thousand years ago,

69
00:04:17,375 --> 00:04:21,461
our ancestors reached the eastern
shore of the Mediterranean.

70
00:04:21,528 --> 00:04:26,367
Then their trail goes cold,
and the Neanderthals move back in.

71
00:04:26,487 --> 00:04:32,497
And fifty-four thousand years ago, a small
group of us, armed with bows and arrows,

72
00:04:32,564 --> 00:04:36,787
reached a rock shelter in southern France:
Grotte Mandrin.

73
00:04:36,945 --> 00:04:38,647
They soon vanished.

74
00:04:38,714 --> 00:04:43,937
The Neanderthals took the shelter back,
and held it for thousands of years.

75
00:04:44,166 --> 00:04:46,945
So we didn't arrive as conquerors.

76
00:04:47,012 --> 00:04:49,945
We arrived... and we lost.

77
00:04:50,012 --> 00:04:51,774
Again and again.

78
00:04:52,311 --> 00:04:57,150
Until, around fifty thousand years ago,
we came once more.

79
00:04:57,311 --> 00:05:01,381
A bigger wave. And this time, we stayed.

80
00:05:01,507 --> 00:05:06,192
And this time, when we met them,
we didn't just pass each other by.

81
00:05:06,354 --> 00:05:08,500
We had children together.

82
00:05:09,128 --> 00:05:12,985
How do we know?
Because of a man in Leipzig, Germany,

83
00:05:13,052 --> 00:05:17,600
who spent decades on something
most scientists thought impossible:

84
00:05:17,667 --> 00:05:22,505
reading DNA from bones
tens of thousands of years old.

85
00:05:22,583 --> 00:05:27,901
In 2010, Svante Pääbo's team
read the DNA of a Neanderthal,

86
00:05:27,968 --> 00:05:30,730
and compared it with people alive today.

87
00:05:30,812 --> 00:05:34,515
People from Europe
and Asia were carrying pieces of it.

88
00:05:34,582 --> 00:05:38,497
That work won him the Nobel Prize in 2022.

89
00:05:38,627 --> 00:05:43,214
The latest research puts
most of this mixing in one long chapter,

90
00:05:43,281 --> 00:05:48,773
beginning around fifty thousand years ago
and lasting about seven thousand years.

91
00:05:48,901 --> 00:05:54,219
In Romania, a jaw from about forty
thousand years ago belonged to a man

92
00:05:54,286 --> 00:05:58,911
with a Neanderthal ancestor
just four to six generations back.

93
00:05:58,978 --> 00:06:01,970
Something
like his great-great-grandfather.

94
00:06:02,196 --> 00:06:05,667
And in a cave in Siberia,
scientists tested

95
00:06:05,734 --> 00:06:11,650
more than two thousand scraps of bone,
one by one, looking for a human.

96
00:06:11,776 --> 00:06:14,076
Only one was human.

97
00:06:14,304 --> 00:06:17,699
In 2018, they read its DNA.

98
00:06:17,766 --> 00:06:21,007
It belonged to a girl at least
thirteen years old,

99
00:06:21,074 --> 00:06:24,220
who died about ninety thousand years ago.

100
00:06:24,349 --> 00:06:28,726
Her mother was a Neanderthal.
Her father, a Denisovan.

101
00:06:29,114 --> 00:06:32,337
Two kinds of humans... and a daughter.

102
00:06:32,972 --> 00:06:37,520
Scientists call her Denny.
And finding a child like her by pure

103
00:06:37,587 --> 00:06:43,195
chance suggests that when different
humans met, this probably wasn't rare.

104
00:06:43,423 --> 00:06:47,049
But wait. Denny's father was a Denisovan.

105
00:06:47,116 --> 00:06:51,108
The people whose first
trace was a sliver of a fingertip.

106
00:06:51,237 --> 00:06:56,863
In 2008, in that same cave,
a tiny bone turned up:

107
00:06:56,930 --> 00:07:01,786
the tip of a girl's little finger.
Its DNA wasn't ours.

108
00:07:01,853 --> 00:07:05,922
It wasn't Neanderthal.
It was a new kind of human.

109
00:07:06,049 --> 00:07:09,674
They were named after the cave:
the Denisovans.

110
00:07:09,741 --> 00:07:11,059
A ghost.

111
00:07:11,126 --> 00:07:14,579
We had their DNA, but not their face.

112
00:07:14,808 --> 00:07:18,801
Then, slowly, the ghost took shape.

113
00:07:18,932 --> 00:07:24,480
In 1980, a Buddhist monk meditating
in a cave on the Tibetan Plateau,

114
00:07:24,547 --> 00:07:28,173
more than ten thousand feet up,
found a jaw.

115
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:31,847
In 2019, it turned out to be Denisovan.

116
00:07:31,977 --> 00:07:38,025
In 2025, another jaw, dragged
up from the seabed off Taiwan by fishing

117
00:07:38,092 --> 00:07:42,969
nets and sold in an antique shop,
turned out to be Denisovan too.

118
00:07:43,197 --> 00:07:46,420
And that same year... a skull.

119
00:07:46,652 --> 00:07:52,508
In 1933, in the Chinese city of Harbin,
under Japanese occupation,

120
00:07:52,575 --> 00:07:57,123
a laborer building a bridge found it,
and hid it at the bottom of an abandoned

121
00:07:57,190 --> 00:08:00,029
well, to keep it from the occupiers.

122
00:08:00,159 --> 00:08:04,689
Eighty-five years later, on his deathbed,
he told his family.

123
00:08:04,928 --> 00:08:06,766
And they went to the well.

124
00:08:06,996 --> 00:08:10,313
In 2025, proteins from the bone,

125
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:16,911
and DNA from the plaque on its teeth,
gave the answer: this was a Denisovan too.

126
00:08:17,134 --> 00:08:22,511
Almost a century after that well,
the ghost finally had a face.

127
00:08:23,244 --> 00:08:27,484
So if we met, and had children together...

128
00:08:27,551 --> 00:08:29,390
why did they disappear?

129
00:08:29,614 --> 00:08:31,761
Let's look at the suspects.

130
00:08:31,848 --> 00:08:35,225
Suspect one: us, with spears.

131
00:08:35,463 --> 00:08:37,781
It's the picture many of us carry.

132
00:08:37,848 --> 00:08:43,994
But in all those bones, there's almost no
clear evidence of war between us and them.

133
00:08:44,166 --> 00:08:46,945
Suspect two: the climate.

134
00:08:47,012 --> 00:08:51,715
About forty thousand years ago,
Europe was hit by brutal cold,

135
00:08:51,782 --> 00:08:54,945
and near today's Naples,
a volcano erupted,

136
00:08:55,012 --> 00:08:59,774
one of the largest the region
had seen in two hundred thousand years.

137
00:08:59,854 --> 00:09:04,846
But Neanderthals had survived ice
ages for hundreds of thousands of years.

138
00:09:05,084 --> 00:09:09,077
Climate may have pushed them.
It doesn't explain everything.

139
00:09:09,248 --> 00:09:13,643
Suspect three is the quietest:
their own numbers.

140
00:09:13,710 --> 00:09:16,548
And the clue is in their DNA.

141
00:09:16,728 --> 00:09:19,584
A Neanderthal woman from Siberia:

142
00:09:19,651 --> 00:09:23,643
her parents were as closely
related as half-siblings.

143
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:26,422
A Neanderthal family from another cave,

144
00:09:26,489 --> 00:09:29,268
a father and his teenage
daughter among them,

145
00:09:29,335 --> 00:09:32,404
lived in a group of perhaps ten or twenty.

146
00:09:32,483 --> 00:09:37,570
And in 2024,
the DNA of a Neanderthal nicknamed Thorin,

147
00:09:37,637 --> 00:09:41,454
from that same French shelter,
showed his people had been cut off

148
00:09:41,521 --> 00:09:45,898
from other Neanderthals for around fifty
thousand years.

149
00:09:45,965 --> 00:09:49,650
Even though some lived just ten
days' walk away.

150
00:09:49,877 --> 00:09:54,793
Small groups. Few children.
Very few new faces.

151
00:09:55,220 --> 00:09:57,058
Now look at us.

152
00:09:57,262 --> 00:09:59,964
Even three hundred thousand years ago,

153
00:10:00,031 --> 00:10:03,080
in Kenya,
people at the dawn of our species

154
00:10:03,147 --> 00:10:08,716
were carrying black volcanic glass from as
far as fifty-five miles away.

155
00:10:08,794 --> 00:10:13,112
And fifty thousand years ago,
people in Africa living nearly

156
00:10:13,179 --> 00:10:19,054
two thousand miles apart made almost
identical beads from ostrich eggshell.

157
00:10:19,121 --> 00:10:24,498
A network of strangers, passing
things and ideas from hand to hand.

158
00:10:24,755 --> 00:10:28,516
Many researchers now
think this was the real difference.

159
00:10:28,601 --> 00:10:31,978
Not bigger brains... more connections.

160
00:10:32,191 --> 00:10:36,586
When one of our groups had a bad year,
there were others to turn to.

161
00:10:36,653 --> 00:10:40,414
When one group invented something,
the idea spread.

162
00:10:40,491 --> 00:10:45,330
And a small, isolated population
doesn't need a war to disappear.

163
00:10:45,568 --> 00:10:51,176
Computer models show that a few bad years,
with too few babies, can be enough.

164
00:10:51,505 --> 00:10:55,746
But the biggest surprise in this story
isn't how they disappeared.

165
00:10:55,813 --> 00:10:57,497
It's where they went.

166
00:10:58,228 --> 00:11:02,220
Some of them never disappeared at all.
They joined us.

167
00:11:02,448 --> 00:11:05,919
Every time a Neanderthal
had a child with one of us,

168
00:11:05,986 --> 00:11:08,150
a little of them crossed over.

169
00:11:08,217 --> 00:11:13,440
And because there were so many more of us,
those children grew up in our world.

170
00:11:13,567 --> 00:11:17,654
Generation after generation,
their people faded.

171
00:11:17,721 --> 00:11:21,791
But their DNA kept traveling, inside us.

172
00:11:22,018 --> 00:11:25,913
Today, if your ancestors
came from outside Africa,

173
00:11:25,980 --> 00:11:29,567
about 2% of your DNA is Neanderthal.

174
00:11:29,634 --> 00:11:33,259
Even people with only African
ancestry carry a little,

175
00:11:33,326 --> 00:11:36,472
because some of our ancestors
later went back.

176
00:11:36,544 --> 00:11:40,631
And because everyone carries
different pieces, scientists have found

177
00:11:40,698 --> 00:11:46,152
at least a fifth of the Neanderthal genome
scattered across people alive today.

178
00:11:46,326 --> 00:11:49,857
And these pieces aren't junk.
They still work.

179
00:11:50,036 --> 00:11:53,182
Remember the Denisovans
on the Tibetan Plateau?

180
00:11:53,344 --> 00:11:58,123
Today, Tibetans carry a version of a gene
from them that helps their bodies

181
00:11:58,190 --> 00:12:02,336
cope with the thin air,
thirteen thousand feet up.

182
00:12:02,407 --> 00:12:06,801
Part of your immune system's
front line came from Neanderthals:

183
00:12:06,868 --> 00:12:12,245
genes that had been fighting the germs
of Europe and Asia long before we arrived.

184
00:12:12,376 --> 00:12:16,155
And in 2020,
in the middle of the COVID pandemic,

185
00:12:16,222 --> 00:12:20,599
a fifty-thousand-year-old
inheritance suddenly mattered.

186
00:12:20,676 --> 00:12:26,071
A stretch of Neanderthal DNA, carried
by about half the people of South Asia,

187
00:12:26,138 --> 00:12:28,840
raised the risk of severe COVID.

188
00:12:28,907 --> 00:12:29,994
By one estimate,

189
00:12:30,061 --> 00:12:34,438
carriers were up to three times
more likely to need a ventilator.

190
00:12:34,513 --> 00:12:36,754
And another Neanderthal piece,

191
00:12:36,821 --> 00:12:41,890
carried by almost half of people
outside Africa, lowered that risk.

192
00:12:42,023 --> 00:12:47,400
Their DNA is still,
inside us, a matter of life and death.

193
00:12:47,905 --> 00:12:52,376
And there are places in your DNA
where nothing of them survived at all.

194
00:12:52,443 --> 00:12:56,128
Long stretches, as if kept for us alone.

195
00:12:56,207 --> 00:13:01,430
One of those empty stretches holds a gene
linked to speech and language.

196
00:13:01,558 --> 00:13:05,414
Scientists are now studying
these empty places closely,

197
00:13:05,481 --> 00:13:10,089
because part of what made us
different may be written right there.

198
00:13:10,819 --> 00:13:13,273
So... why us?

199
00:13:13,708 --> 00:13:16,272
Strength? They had more.

200
00:13:16,339 --> 00:13:19,887
Brains? Theirs were as big. Skill?

201
00:13:19,954 --> 00:13:23,562
Their hands made glue,
and string, and art.

202
00:13:23,793 --> 00:13:27,247
The best answer
we have today is a humbler one.

203
00:13:27,562 --> 00:13:30,495
There were more of us. We were connected:

204
00:13:30,562 --> 00:13:35,726
a species that kept walking toward
strangers and turning them into partners.

205
00:13:35,793 --> 00:13:37,708
And we got lucky.

206
00:13:37,887 --> 00:13:40,513
And when we finally met the others...

207
00:13:40,580 --> 00:13:44,243
we didn't just take their place.
We took some of them...

208
00:13:44,310 --> 00:13:45,418
with us.

209
00:13:45,839 --> 00:13:48,003
Even then, it was close.

210
00:13:48,070 --> 00:13:52,003
The first people of our kind in Europe
whose DNA we've read,

211
00:13:52,070 --> 00:13:58,062
a few relatives in Germany and Czechia,
lived about forty-five thousand years ago.

212
00:13:58,144 --> 00:14:01,269
Today, they have no descendants at all.

213
00:14:01,336 --> 00:14:02,905
They vanished too.

214
00:14:03,230 --> 00:14:07,317
If Homo erectus's
time on Earth were one whole day,

215
00:14:07,384 --> 00:14:10,838
ours so far wouldn't
even reach four hours.

216
00:14:10,963 --> 00:14:13,879
We're
not the champions of the human family.

217
00:14:14,040 --> 00:14:16,186
We're just its newest member.

218
00:14:16,776 --> 00:14:19,383
Look at his face... again.

219
00:14:19,625 --> 00:14:23,925
He isn't a stranger from a lost world.
He's family.

220
00:14:24,352 --> 00:14:28,785
Seven kinds of humans disappeared.
Some of them...

221
00:14:28,852 --> 00:14:30,959
disappeared into us.

222
00:14:31,784 --> 00:14:36,217
If you could sit by a fire one night,
with one of those seven...

223
00:14:36,284 --> 00:14:39,623
which would you choose?
Tell us in the comments.

224
00:14:40,054 --> 00:14:44,508
Odyssey Beyond. Curiosity has no limits.

